From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 15:47:26 +0300
Subject: nv30: indices the CPU holds go to the GPU through the upload buffer
 (NV30_INDEX_UPLOAD)

Index data the GPU can't fetch from an index buffer was packed into the push buffer word by
word: glDrawElements from client memory (OpenArena's vanilla renderer, every surface: 32-bit
indices, one or two per word) and 8-bit indices from a buffer object (the RSX's index fetch
has 16- and 32-bit formats only; nv30 claims R8 index buffers so u_vbuf doesn't rewrite them,
and the CPU reads them from nouveau_buffer's cached copy, read back from VRAM once per
buffer, not per draw). Every index then crossed the CPU twice more: packed by Mesa, and
copied by ps3rsx into its ring.

Now, from NV30_INDEX_UPLOAD indices up (32 by default on the PS3; 0 restores the inline
pushes, the default elsewhere; read once at screen creation), they are copied once into the
stream upload buffer (GART, persistently mapped: u_upload_alloc is a pointer bump), 16- and
32-bit indices with memcpy, 8-bit ones widened to 16 bits, and the GPU fetches them like an
index buffer: IDXBUF_OFFSET/IDXBUF_FORMAT (location GART) and VB_INDEX_BATCH words, a dozen
words whatever the count. The upload buffer is referenced before validation (fenced, in the
kernel's list, like 0028's index buffers). Shorter arrays stay inline, where the few words
cost less than the reference.

Changes the command stream for such draws: IDXBUF_OFFSET, IDXBUF_FORMAT with the GART bit
and VB_INDEX_BATCH instead of VB_ELEMENT_U16/U32 bursts. All are in ps3rsx's allowlist with
no data checks, and the RSX's index fetch from GART (DMA_VTXBUF1, the IO window) is the path
u_vbuf's uploaded client vertex arrays already take. Restart indices are unchanged by the
widening (an 8-bit restart index compares equal as 16 bits).

Forwarded: no

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nv30_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nv30_screen.c
